I don't understand why "Boolean" operations are not supported for fix-sized arrays. Consider the following equivalent three functions:
@jax.jit
def f1(x, a, b):
    return jax.lax.cond(a < x <= b, 
                        lambda _: 666, 
                        lambda _: 999, 
                        None)

@jax.jit
def f2(x, a, b):
    return jax.lax.cond((x > a) and (x <= b), 
                        lambda _: 666, 
                        lambda _: 999, 
                        None)

@jax.jit
def f3(x, a, b):
    return jax.lax.cond((x > a) & (x <= b), 
                        lambda _: 666, 
                        lambda _: 999, 
                        None)

x, a, b = jnp.array(1.), 2., 3.
f1(x, a, b)
f2(x, a, b)
f3(x, a, b)
f1 and f2 are equivalent, and they will raise error jax.errors.TracerBoolConversionError: Attempted boolean conversion of traced array with shape bool[].
However, f3 will work, since & is a bit operation not boolean. Since bit is equivalent to bool when the operands are bool, are the different results above intended? Also I don't understand why the bool operation would fail in this case, as they are indeed not dynamic.
